WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.772
Authentication proves who a user is, while authorization decides what that user can do.

2
00:00:05.772 --> 00:00:17.780
Many serious access control failures happen after login, when the application trusts a role, identifier, route, or client-side decision that should have been enforced on the server.

3
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:24.017
Privilege escalation occurs when a user gains abilities meant for a higher-privileged account.

4
00:00:24.017 --> 00:00:35.295
Vertical escalation might let a normal user perform administrator functions, while horizontal escalation might let one customer view or change another customer's records.

5
00:00:35.295 --> 00:00:49.957
Insecure direct object references happen when an application exposes record identifiers, file names, tenant IDs, or other object references without checking whether the current user is allowed to access the target object.

6
00:00:49.957 --> 00:00:54.535
APIs require the same access control discipline as browser workflows.

7
00:00:54.535 --> 00:01:02.298
A hidden button or disabled menu item is not a security boundary if the underlying API accepts unauthorized requests.

8
00:01:02.298 --> 00:01:06.345
Deny by default and require explicit authorization decisions.

9
00:01:06.345 --> 00:01:10.657
Perform server-side checks for every sensitive action and object.

10
00:01:10.657 --> 00:01:15.367
Apply least privilege to users, services, and administrative functions.

11
00:01:15.367 --> 00:01:26.314
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include AC-2 - Account Management; AC-3 - Access Enforcement; AC-6 - Least Privilege; IA-2 - Identification and Authentication.

